The '''2018 Northcote by-election''' was a [[List of New Zealand by-elections|New Zealand by-election]] that was held in {{NZ electorate link|Northcote}} on 9 June 2018.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/102954151/northcote-byelection-date-announced |title=Northcote by-election date announced |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=9 April 2018 |accessdate=9 April 2018}}</ref> The seat became vacant on 15 April 2018, following the resignation of then-Member for Northcote [[Jonathan Coleman (politician)|Jonathan Coleman]],<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/hansard-debates/rhr/combined/HansD_20180329_20180329 | title = Thursday, 29 March 2018 – Volume 728 ''(Hansard)'' | website = parliament.nz | author = New Zealand Parliament | accessdate = 29 March 2018 }}</ref> a member of the [[New Zealand National Party]].<ref>{{cite web | url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/102483343/nationals-jonathan-coleman-calls-time-on-parliament-sparking-a-byelection-in-northcote | title= National's Jonathan Coleman calls time on Parliament, sparking a by-election in Northcote | date= 22 March 2018 | accessdate= 22 March 2018 | first1= Stacy |first2 = Jo | last1= Kirk | last2= Moir}}</ref>

National Party candidate [[Dan Bidois]] retained the seat for National, but with a significantly reduced majority blamed on low voter turnout; it was therefore described as a victory for the National Party Leader [[Simon Bridges]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/opinion/104590593/northcote-byelection-no-northland-as-both-national-and-labour-can-claim-victory|title=Northcote byelection no Northland as both National and Labour can claim victory|publisher=Stuff (Fairfax)|date=9 June 2018}}</ref>

==Background==
The Northcote electorate is based around the southern and western suburbs of Auckland's [[North Shore, New Zealand|North Shore]]. In addition to the eponymous [[Northcote, Auckland|Northcote]], there are [[Birkenhead, New Zealand|Birkenhead]], [[Birkdale, New Zealand|Birkdale]], [[Beach Haven, New Zealand|Beach Haven]] and the southern end of [[Glenfield, New Zealand|Glenfield]]. It was created ahead of the change to [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) voting in [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]] by merging the seat of [[Birkenhead (New Zealand electorate)|Birkenhead]] with most of the old [[Glenfield (New Zealand electorate)|Glenfield]] electorate. A small boundary adjustment was done prior to the {{NZ election link|1999}}, but no further boundary adjustments were undertaken in the subsequent redistributions in 2002,<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2002 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2002%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=0-478-20169-9 |publisher=Representation Commission |accessdate=4 October 2014 |page=8 |date=21 March 2002 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006153405/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2002%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> 2007,<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2007 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |accessdate=4 October 2014 |page=8 |date=14 September 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190123193826/https://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |archive-date=23 January 2019 |url-status=dead }}</ref> and 2013/14.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |accessdate=4 October 2014 |page=8 |date=4 April 2014 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075102/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |archivedate=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Northcote continues the electoral habits of its predecessor seats; Birkenhead was a reasonably safe seat for the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]], supplying it with [[Jim McLay]], who led the party in the mid-1980s. In [[1987 New Zealand general election|1987]], the seat that would provide National with a leader provided [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] with a gain, before swinging back into the blue column when Labour's fortunes thinned out at the [[1990 New Zealand general election|1990 election]]. Glenfield also followed this boom and bust model, being held by Labour Party Member [[Judy Keall]] through the duration of the fourth Labour government before the National Party landslide in 1990 claimed Keall as one of its victims.

===Outgoing member===
At the [[2005 New Zealand general election|2005 election]], [[New Zealand National Party|National Party's]] [[Jonathan Coleman (politician)|Jonathan Coleman]] secured the seat, defeating [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour's]] [[Ann Hartley]]. Coleman went on to be re-elected at the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008]], [[2011 New Zealand general election|2011]], [[2014 New Zealand general election|2014]] and [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017]] elections. At the 2017 election, he secured a majority of 6,210. When [[New Zealand National Party|National]] formed a [[Fifth National Government of New Zealand|government]] after the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 election]], Coleman was appointed as [[Minister of Broadcasting (New Zealand)|Minister of Broadcasting]] and [[Minister of Immigration (New Zealand)|Minister of Immigration]]; after the [[2011 New Zealand general election|2011 election]] he was promoted to be [[Minister of Defence (New Zealand)|Minister of Defence]] for the government's second term. Following the government's [[2014 New Zealand general election|re-election in 2014]], Coleman became [[Minister of Health (New Zealand)|Minister of Health]]—ultimately his most senior role—which he held until the [[2017 New Zealand general election|government's defeat in the 2017 election]]. Coleman contested for the leadership of the National Party in the [[2016 New Zealand National Party leadership election|2016 leadership election]], which was won by [[Bill English]].

On 22 March 2018, Coleman announced he would resign from [[New Zealand Parliament|Parliament]], triggering the by-election. It was the first by-election of the [[52nd New Zealand Parliament|52nd Parliament]]. On 29 March, it was confirmed that his resignation would take effect at "midnight on 15 April 2018".<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/hansard-debates/rhr/combined/HansD_20180329_20180329 | title = Thursday, 29 March 2018 – Volume 728 ''(Hansard)'' | website = parliament.nz | author = New Zealand Parliament | accessdate = 29 March 2018 }}</ref>

