==Candidates==
Labour's 2017 general election candidate [[Shanan Halbert]] stated his intention to contest his party's nomination again.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/102499333/byelection-labour-candidate-shanan-halbert-keen-to-run-in-northcote-again |title=Labour candidate Shanan Halbert keen to run in Northcote again |date=22 March 2018 |accessdate=22 March 2018 |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |first1=Henry |last1=Cooke }}</ref> On 10 April Labour released the three nomination candidates; Halbert, Paul McGreal and [[Auckland Council]]lor [[Richard Hills (politician)|Richard Hills]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=12029930 |title=Labour announces potential Northcote byelection candidates |date=10 April 2018 |accessdate=10 April 2018 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] }}</ref> Shanan Halbert was chosen as Labour's candidate.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=12033098 |title=Shanan Halbert Labour's pick for Northcote byelection |date=15 April 2018 |accessdate=15 April 2018 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/auckland/local-news/north-shore-times/103492682/labours-northcote-candidate-shanan-halbert-and-nationals-dan-bidois-both-kiwi-battlers |title= Labours Northcote candidate Shanan Halbert and Nationals Dan Bidois both kiwi battlers   |publisher= Stuff (Fairfax) |date= 1 May 2018}}</ref>

Former Green Party candidate [[Vernon Tava]] confirmed he was seeking the National Party nomination.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=12023377 |title=Former Green activist Vernon Tava seeking National nomination in Northcote |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=30 March 2018 |accessdate=31 March 2018 }}</ref> Tava, a member of the [[Waitematā Local Board]], stood in Northcote for the Greens in 2011 and unsuccessfully contested the co-leadership [[2015 Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand male co-leadership election|in 2015]]. He later left the party believing the Greens were becoming "too socialist".<ref>{{cite news |url=http://politik.co.nz/en/content/politics/1038/Top-Green-resigns-and-says-party-has-become-socialist-Vernon-Tava-Greens.htm |title=Top Green resigns and says party has become socialist |work=Politik |date=28 February 2017 |accessdate=28 February 2017 |archive-date=11 April 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170411031310/https://www.politik.co.nz/en/content/politics/1038/Top-Green-resigns-and-says-party-has-become-socialist-Vernon-Tava-Greens.htm |url-status=dead }}</ref> Tava did not make National's shortlist. The five candidates who did were economist Dan Bidois, [[Kaipātiki Local Board]] chair Danielle Grant, Darren Ward, [[Upper Harbour Local Board]] member Lisa Whyte, and [[Simon Watts]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/102976901/green-turned-national-vernon-tava-knocked-out-of-candidate-selection-for-northcote |title=Green turned National Vernon Tava knocked out of candidate selection for Northcote |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] | date=10 April 2018 | accessdate=10 April 2018 }}</ref> Dan Bidois was selected as National's candidate.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.radionz.co.nz/news/political/355118/national-selects-northcote-by-election-candidate |title=National selects Northcote by-election candidate |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] | date=15 April 2018 | accessdate=15 April 2018 }}</ref>

Perennial candidate Peter Wakeman offered himself to the electorate, but later withdrew. After quitting he endorsed Democrats candidate Tricia Cheel.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/AK1805/S00250/wakeman-quits-northcote-by-election-9-june-2018.htm |title=Wakeman quits Northcote by-election 9 June 2018 |work=[[Scoop.co.nz]] | date=11 May 2018 | accessdate=11 May 2018 }}</ref>

The [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] also decided to field a candidate in the by-election. Co-leader [[Marama Davidson]] said "The Northcote by-election is an opportunity for the Green Party to promote our early successes in Government while highlighting our points of difference".<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA1804/S00260/green-party-to-stand-candidate-in-northcote-by-election.htm |title=Green Party to stand candidate in Northcote by-election |work=[[Scoop.co.nz]] | date=24 April 2018 | accessdate=24 April 2018 }}</ref> Rebekah Jaung was announced as the party's candidate on 11 May 2018.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/103822324/northcote-byelection-greens-select-rebekah-jaung-as-candidate|title=Northcote by-election: Greens select Rebekah Jaung as candidate|date=11 May 2018|publisher=Stuff.co.nz}}</ref>

[[New Zealand First]] announced that they would not contest the by-election. New Zealand First's candidate from 2017, Kym Koloni, decided to stand as an independent.<ref name="among"/>

On 6 May 2018, [[ACT New Zealand|ACT]] leader [[David Seymour (New Zealand politician)|David Seymour]] announced that [[Stephen Berry (politician)|Stephen Berry]] was the party's candidate.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA1805/S00066/act-selects-stephen-berry-for-northcote.htm|accessdate=6 May 2018|title=ACT selects Stephen Berry for Northcote|date=6 May 2018|publisher=Scoop.co.nz}}</ref>

